1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,483
India's biggest weather network already exists.

2
00:00:02,483 --> 00:00:08,690
It has lakhs of stations, it covers every district, and it's switched on right now.

3
00:00:08,690 --> 00:00:11,586
Nobody noticed — because we call it Jio.

4
00:00:12,286 --> 00:00:16,010
Weather in India is hyperlocal, but our data isn't.

5
00:00:16,010 --> 00:00:23,872
On 26 July 2005, Santacruz in Mumbai got 944 millimetres of rain.

6
00:00:23,872 --> 00:00:27,183
Colaba, twenty kilometres away, got 73.

7
00:00:27,183 --> 00:00:30,079
About forty radars can't see that difference.

8
00:00:30,079 --> 00:00:35,045
The people who lose the most — riders, vendors, labourers — get no help,

9
00:00:35,045 --> 00:00:37,114
and insurance claims take months.

10
00:00:37,814 --> 00:00:39,055
Here's the insight.

11
00:00:39,055 --> 00:00:41,952
Rain weakens the radio links between towers —

12
00:00:41,952 --> 00:00:47,745
and how much it weakens tells you how hard it's raining, minute by minute.

13
00:00:47,745 --> 00:00:50,641
So every tower is a rain gauge.

14
00:00:50,641 --> 00:00:53,952
We don't build sensors. We become the sensor.

15
00:00:54,652 --> 00:00:57,548
Does it actually work? We built it.

16
00:00:57,548 --> 00:01:04,169
On 500 real microwave links, our rain readings matched national radar at 0.95.

17
00:01:04,169 --> 00:01:09,962
Our payout rule made the same decision as radar ninety-five percent of the time.

18
00:01:09,962 --> 00:01:14,100
And twenty-five years of IMD records show where to start:

19
00:01:14,100 --> 00:01:16,997
Mumbai gets twelve heavy-rain days a year.

20
00:01:17,697 --> 00:01:18,524
Meet JioMausam —

21
00:01:18,524 --> 00:01:22,248
a live, street-level weather map built from network data.

22
00:01:22,248 --> 00:01:24,731
On top of it: Mausam Kavach.

23
00:01:24,731 --> 00:01:27,628
Delivery apps buy it for their riders.

24
00:01:27,628 --> 00:01:33,421
When the towers around a rider measure ten millimetres of rain in three hours,

25
00:01:33,421 --> 00:01:37,972
three hundred rupees lands in the rider's JioPay wallet within hours.

26
00:01:37,972 --> 00:01:40,869
No claim forms. No surveyor. No waiting.

27
00:01:41,569 --> 00:01:43,224
It pays three ways.

28
00:01:43,224 --> 00:01:50,672
Delivery apps buy Kavach Plus: fifty thousand Mumbai riders is about twelve crore rupees of premiums a year.

29
00:01:50,672 --> 00:01:58,948
Later, workers add Kavach Basic at recharge — one percent of Jio's users is 174 crore in premiums.

30
00:01:58,948 --> 00:02:04,328
Jio earns fees on both, and sells the data to insurers and states.

31
00:02:05,028 --> 00:02:06,269
Why only Reliance?

32
00:02:06,269 --> 00:02:08,752
A startup can't own the towers.

33
00:02:08,752 --> 00:02:11,648
An insurer can't reach 500 million people.

34
00:02:11,648 --> 00:02:17,028
No other telecom has a payments app, an insurance arm, a retail chain

35
00:02:17,028 --> 00:02:20,338
and a giga-scale energy business under one roof.

36
00:02:20,338 --> 00:02:21,993
Reliance has every layer.

37
00:02:22,693 --> 00:02:29,728
Phase one: a ninety-day Mumbai coverage audit, with payouts in shadow mode — rain, riders, one delivery app.

38
00:02:29,728 --> 00:02:35,521
Phase two: real payouts in the IRDAI sandbox, and heatwaves in Nagpur and Ahmedabad.

39
00:02:35,521 --> 00:02:40,486
Phase three: Kavach Basic at every recharge. Phase four: a national map.

40
00:02:40,486 --> 00:02:45,038
India won't adapt to climate change only by building new infrastructure.

41
00:02:45,038 --> 00:02:49,176
It'll adapt by listening to the infrastructure it already has.

42
00:02:49,176 --> 00:02:51,659
This is JioMausam. Different by design.
